# DESIGN.md; docs/multicolumn-plan.md. WHICH WIDTHS THIS SCENE MAY
# SAMPLE: each platform's component draws the one-pane/two-pane line
# somewhere else (GNOME collapses below 400sp, Material wants 840dp,
# TwoPaneView sits between), so a width inside that band is legitimately
# one pane on one platform and two on another. Only 360 and 900 are
# below and above EVERY boundary; check-steps' band rule holds it.
expect_entries 0
expect_title "split"
resize_window 900x600
expect_split "regular/split"
click button#0
expect_entries 1
expect label#0 "list pane"
expect label#last "detail pane"
expect_split "regular/split"
# BOTH PANES AT ONCE, and only these two lines can say it: the model
# reads and expect_split both pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing.
expect_ax label#0 "label/list pane"
expect_ax label#last "label/detail pane"
# THE BACK RULE: with both panes on screen the affordance has nothing to
# reveal and does not pop — every platform refuses here already, and
# popping would leave a blank detail pane.
back
expect_entries 1
expect_split "regular/split"
resize_window 360x600
expect_split "compact/stacked"
expect_entries 1
# Collapsed, the detail COVERS the list, so back pops again: list-detail
# is a PRESENTATION of the stack, not a second lifecycle.
back
expect_entries 0
expect label#0 "popped detail"
resize_window 900x600
expect_split "regular/split"
